The inclusive-shadow policy avoided **2,510,023 of 2,849,120 potential
decode-demotion writes (88.1%)** across these samples. This counter excludes
initial prompt placement in the slow tier. It is a simulated event ratio,
not a measured STT-RAM write rate or a paired no-shadow experiment: the
"potential" count assumes the same promotion/eviction sequence without
reusing an existing backup.
Each count is one layer-token (4 KiB bf16 K/V), so the saved payload is
**10.28 GB across 135 requests**, or 72.6 MiB/request. At the model's assumed
12.5 GB/s write bandwidth, that is only **0.154 ms of ideal write-transfer
time per generated token**; at its assumed 8 pJ/bit it is 0.658 J across the
run, less than 1% of modeled decode dynamic energy. The same prompt lengths
would initially place about 25.90 GB into the hypothetical slow tier, so the
avoided fraction of *potential total slow-tier writes including prefill* is
about 27.4%. These are derived counterfactuals, not hardware measurements.
Likewise, the current analytical energy counters cover decode KV traffic;
they omit prompt placement, model compute, and system power and are not
whole-request energy measurements.

