**Corrected latency and peak memory.** `results/latency_headline.json` timed the model forward pass but excluded cache-policy work, so its 25.1 ms/token figure is **not** an end-to-end inter-token latency. The corrected-control real-text, full-loop matrix measured **115.70 ms/token for TieredKV versus 34.42 ms/token for SnapKV at 16k prompt tokens** on L40S, with 50 fixed decode tokens and three measured trials (`results/remediation_v3/latency.json`). Moving small promotion-hysteresis metadata off GPU reduced TieredKV's earlier 149.93 ms/token measurement, but policy overhead remains substantial. All methods build the full prompt KV before compressing it, so their measured 16k-token peak PyTorch allocation is about **18.59 GB**; this implementation does not yet reduce peak prefill VRAM. STT energy and movement costs remain analytical estimates, not physical measurements.

**Current faithful-baseline check (2026-09-29).** The current-source 135-example upstream-selector SnapKV comparison under a configured 512 MiB maximum final KV payload found TieredKV−SnapKV task-balanced quality of **−0.05 points** (conditional 95% bootstrap interval [−1.36,+1.36]). Under the final boolean-gather and boolean-hot-compaction source, all Tiered predictions, scores, final resident byte counts, and cache counters match the earlier control exactly. The interval does not establish quality superiority or equivalence. On one separate 16K prompt in three same-process ABBA blocks, complete-loop decode measured **82.76 versus 25.77 ms/token** (Tiered/Snap; 3.21× slower), with p95 86.34/26.03 ms and actual final KV payload 425/512 MiB. Tiered had a lower decode allocation peak (15.234/16.522 GB) but a higher prefill peak (18.796/17.958 GB). No joint quality/latency/memory win is established. See `results/baseline_fidelity/snap_exante_full_currenthot_report.json`, `bool_hot_compaction_parity.json`, and `snap_tier_loop_currenthot_report.json`; the latency loop is one prompt, not serving throughput.

**Historical STT bandwidth sensitivity (2026-09-21, analytical replay from saved counters).** Varying the assumed STT bandwidth over 21/5.25, 50/12.5, and 100/25 GB/s moves only TieredKV's analytical bill; baselines never touch STT. The old modeled migration latency was **0.803 / 0.346 / 0.180 s** across those scenarios, while modeled energy was **70.63 J** because pJ/bit did not vary. These are *scenario inputs*, not measured bandwidths of a deployable STT-RAM tier. In particular, [Everspin reports 2.7 GB/s for both reads and writes per 1Gb STT-MRAM device](https://investor.everspin.com/news-releases/news-release-details/next-generation-ibm-flashcore-modules-feature-everspins-persyst); eight such interfaces give a theoretical 21.6 GB/s aggregate in each direction before controller, link, or workload losses. [Li et al.'s 26.7 GB/s](https://doi.org/10.1145/3650729) is an array-level read result for a different near-memory design, and [Melody](https://people.cs.vt.edu/~huaicheng/p/asplos25-melody.pdf) characterizes CXL memory rather than STT-MRAM. Neither establishes the current 50/12.5 GB/s pair or its 4× write asymmetry for this system. The older migration-only replay cannot predict full-loop serving latency; the measured benchmark above is unaffected by these assumed numbers. Per-step records were not exported in the older JSONs, so only the migration slice can be replayed exactly.

**What changed to get here** — `TieredKVPolicy._visible_tuple()` builds the attended set from VRAM + quota-capped live STT-RAM (uniform length across layers for HF's shared causal mask). Promotion is driven by the model's real measured attention on STT-RAM tokens (not a K-as-Q sketch proxy), gated by `resolve_promotions()` hysteresis. A `stt_live_floor` (default 1) keeps one live STT row per layer on both the promote and evict paths so one drained layer can't veto exposure for all 32; unanimous-empty tiers (short docs) count as `stt_unused_steps`, not starvation. An attempt to inject *peeked* STT-RAM content directly into real attention was tried and rolled back: HF shares one causal mask across all 32 layers (rolled back after measuring ~20x promotion collapse). Prior harness bugs fixed along the way: cross-sample stats are accumulated (not last-sample-only), dropped tokens are invalidated without an invented payload read or DRAM migration, STT-RAM figures are cited tentpoles (not hand-picked), checkpoints are fingerprinted including sample counts.

### STT-RAM budget ablation (clean)

VRAM fixed at 1024, STT-RAM swept 512→3072, 5 short tasks × 25 samples, bf16, final code (`results/clean_sweep_stt/`; full numbers in `results/tables/table4_stt_ablation.tex` — view with `experiments/preview_tables.py`).

| STT-RAM budget | 512 | 1024 | 2048 | 3072 |
|---|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|
| Avg F1 | 38.19 | 38.06 | 38.67 | 38.27 |

Flat within noise (±0.6) — the old monotonic 35.77→44.98 curve does not survive clean measurement (3 tasks × 10 samples across mixed revisions). With VRAM fixed and exposure quota-bounded, extra slow-tier capacity beyond working-set needs adds retrievable pool the fixed window cannot surface; per-task directions split (triviaqa falls 70.1→66.6 with bigger STT, hotpotqa rises 37.4→38.9). Reported as measured: capacity without visibility. A newer five-task window-size ablation is summarized above; this older sweep is retained for audit.

### Bifurcation-headroom operating point (new)

Reserving 25% STT headroom (`sttram_bifurcation_frac=0.75`, fixed-sample sweep `results/e2_frac*_short.json`, `figures_final/fig8_frac_ablation.pdf`): write savings 7.2% → **82.9%** at −0.17 avg F1 (38.67→38.51, noise; cost concentrates on multifieldqa −1.9, others flat-or-better). Default `frac=1.0` pre-fills STT at prefill and structurally guarantees ~0% savings — the old 0% was a capacity artifact, proven by the infinite-STT oracle (`results/e1_oracle_short.json`: 77–88% savings, F1 flat). This is the paper's write-savings claim, with its trade-off stated.

