## Hardware & Cost Model

The GPU row uses the **NVIDIA L40S** specification; the STT row is an
unvalidated analytical scenario, not a calibrated second tier:

| Tier | Technology | Read BW | Write BW | Energy |
|------|-----------|---------|---------|--------|
| Tier 1 — VRAM | GDDR6 | 864 GB/s | 864 GB/s | 12 pJ/bit |
| Tier 2 — STT-RAM | STT-MRAM (simulated scenario) | assumed 50 GB/s | assumed 12.5 GB/s | assumed 2 / 8 pJ/bit |

The cost model assumes a 4× write/read bandwidth asymmetry; that ratio is
not established for a GPU-accessible STT tier by the cited device data.
Two 1Gb Everspin chips would supply the configured 256 MiB slow-tier raw
capacity but only 5.4 GB/s theoretical aggregate bandwidth in each
direction before controller and link losses. The assumed 50 GB/s read
scenario would require at least 19 such independently usable chips and is
not capacity matched. See the roadmap for the arithmetic and source links.
An isolated pinned CPU-DRAM↔GPU0 probe measured the transfer component on
this machine (L40S, PCIe Gen4×16, local NUMA): a 4 MiB exposed-KV payload
took 9.38 ms in 1,024 separate 4 KiB copies, 0.299 ms in 32 layer-sized
copies, and 0.171 ms packed. A contiguous 256 MiB H2D copy took 10.02 ms
(26.8 GB/s). These are **not STT-MRAM measurements** or end-to-end TieredKV
latencies; the controller, access pattern, device energy, and overlap remain
unknown. The two-chip Everspin capacity match has a 0.777 ms ideal device
read floor for that 4 MiB payload before link cost. The assumed 50 GB/s
read also exceeds the L40S PCIe Gen4×16 nominal one-way ~32 GB/s limit;
[NVIDIA specifies 64 GB/s bidirectional](https://www.nvidia.com/en-in/data-center/l40s/).
See `results/hardware/host_transfer_report.json` for methods and raw links.
`CostModel.with_stt_bw('commercial_2x1gb_ideal')` reproduces the ideal
5.4/5.4 GB/s bandwidth anchor without changing the historical default;
energy prices remain assumptions. A separate audit removed a phantom
payload-read charge on cold-row drops. The equal-VRAM six-task control had
zero drops, so its old modeled totals are unchanged. The equal-total
GovReport control's 25,920 drops lower its historical aggregate modeled
latency by 2.123 ms and energy by 0.001699 J; exact arithmetic is in
`results/hardware/drop_cost_correction.json`. Measured scores and GPU time
are unaffected.
When a backup survives, re-demotion can reuse it without another write.
The corrected `frac=0.75` run avoided 88.1% of potential decode-demotion
writes; that simulated event ratio excludes initial prompt placement.

